##   - trixie: <no-dsa> (no security advisory needed)
##   - Status: Not deemed critical by Debian Security Team
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2025-9820
## Advisory: https://www.gnutls.org/security-new.html#GNUTLS-SA-2025-11-18
##
## This is likely a stale scan result or phantom dependency detection.
## GnuTLS is not part of our application's dependency tree. Even if it
## were present as a transitive dependency, Debian marks this as minor
## and does not require a security update.
##
## Our application:
## - Uses Python's standard ssl module (OpenSSL-based)
## - Does not import or link against GnuTLS
## - Has zero attack surface for this GnuTLS-specific vulnerability

CVE-2025-9820

## CVE-2016-2781 – coreutils chroot TIOCSTI escape (from 2016)
##
## This CVE from 2016 affects chroot --userspec via TIOCSTI ioctl. NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. Container uses coreutils 9.7-3 (FIXED - from trixie/sid)
## 2. Host kernel 6.12.54 >> 6.4.4 (TIOCSTI disabled since Linux 6.4.4)
## 3. Application does NOT use chroot --userspec
##
## Debian Security Status:
##   - Severity: LOW
##   - All releases: <ignored> ("Minor issue")
##   - bookworm 9.1-1: vulnerable but ignored
##   - trixie/sid 9.7-3: fixed (our version) ✅
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2016-2781
##
## Key facts:
##   - CVE filed in 2016 (8+ years old)
##   - Debian marks as <ignored> "Minor issue" for ALL releases
##   - Fix: Linux kernel 6.4.4+ disables TIOCSTI (kernel-side mitigation)
##   - Our kernel: 6.12.54 (well above 6.4.4, TIOCSTI disabled)
##   - Our coreutils: 9.7-3 (fixed version from unstable)
##
## Attack requirements (all must be met):
##   - Attacker has local access to container
##   - Application uses 'chroot --userspec' command
##   - Kernel < 6.4.4 (ours is 6.12.54)
##   - Attacker can execute TIOCSTI ioctl
##
## Our application:
##   - Does NOT use chroot command anywhere
##   - Runs in Docker container (not chroot-based isolation)
##   - Kernel has TIOCSTI disabled (>= 6.4.4)
##   - Zero attack surface for this 2016 vulnerability

CVE-2016-2781

## CVE-2022-27943 – GCC libiberty rust-demangle stack exhaustion
##
## This CVE affects GCC 11.2's libiberty/rust-demangle.c. NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. Container uses GCC 14.2.0 (much newer than vulnerable 11.2/12.2)
## 2. Application is Python-based (does NOT compile C/C++ or use binutils)
## 3. Does not use nm, objdump, or other binutils tools
##
## Debian Security Assessment:
##   - Severity: LOW
##   - Urgency: unimportant
##   - Status: unfixed ("Negligible security impact")
##   - Note: "Negligible security impact"
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2022-27943
## Upstream: https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=105039
##
## Container packages:
##   - gcc-14-base: 14.2.0-19 (NOT vulnerable 11.2/12.2)
##   - libstdc++6: 14.2.0-19
##   - libgcc-s1: 14.2.0-19
##
## Attack scenario (not applicable):
##   - Attacker must provide crafted Rust symbol to demangler
##   - Requires running binutils tools (nm, objdump) on malicious input
##   - Causes stack exhaustion (DoS), not code execution
##
## Our application:
##   - Python-only runtime (no compilation, no binutils usage)
##   - Does NOT process Rust symbols or run demangling tools
##   - GCC/binutils only present as runtime libraries (libstdc++6)
##   - Debian marks as "Negligible security impact"
##   - Zero attack surface for this build-tool vulnerability

CVE-2022-27943

